And the helper:

```rust
/// Compact per-file signal tag list, e.g. `[churn, size, mut 50%]`.
///
/// Tags name the signals that actually drove this file's score (from
/// `FileRisk.signals`) plus a `mut` tag when the mutation-survival sensor
/// fired (`mutation_survival > 0`). Empty when no signals fired.
fn file_signal_tags(risk: &FileRisk) -> String {
    let mut tags: Vec<String> = risk
        .signals
        .iter()
        .filter_map(|s| match *s {
            "▲churn" => Some("churn"),
            "▲recent" => Some("recent"),
            "▲size" => Some("size"),
            "▲reverts" => Some("revert"),
            "▲low-test" => Some("low-test"),
            "▲coupled" => Some("coupled"),
            "▲mut-surv" => None, // surfaced via the mut tag below
            _ => None,
        })
        .map(str::to_string)
        .collect();
    if risk.mutation_survival > 0.0 {
        let pct = (risk.mutation_survival * 100.0).round() as u64;
        tags.push(format!("mut {pct}%"));
    }
    if tags.is_empty() {
        String::new()
    } else {
        format!("[{tags}]")
    }
}
```

Note `format!("[{tags}]")` — tags is Vec<String> and Display joins with commas automatically via the format args. Yes, in Rust 2021, `{}` on a `Vec<String>` prints `[a, b, c]` using the slice's Debug-like Display. Actually `Vec<T>: Display` prints items joined by ", " enclosed in brackets. So `format!("[{tags}]")` would give `[[churn, size]]` — double brackets! Careful.

`format!("{}", vec!["a","b"])` → `[a, b]`. So I should NOT add my own brackets. Just `format!("{tags}")` gives `[churn, size]` already. Let me verify: `Vec<String>` implements `Display` via `impl<T: Display> Display for [T]` and for `Vec<T>` there's `impl Display` in alloc? Actually there's `impl<T: fmt::Display> fmt::Display for Vec<T>` in std (via slice). Yes — `println!("{}", vec![1,2,3])` prints `[1, 2, 3]`. Good. So `tags.to_string()` works directly.

